# Style Library

Read when proposing style options during the interview, or writing format constraints into page prompts.

---

## Line weight and detail by audience

Detail density is the single biggest determinant of whether a coloring book is usable. A four-year-old handed an adult mandala colors two shapes and quits; an adult handed a toddler page feels patronised.

| Audience | Line weight | Distinct regions per page | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ages 2–4 | Very thick, 6–8pt | 5–10 | Huge simple shapes, one subject centred, generous white space |
| Ages 5–7 | Thick, 4–5pt | 10–25 | Simple background, friendly rounded forms |
| Ages 8–12 | Medium, 2–3pt | 25–60 | Real backgrounds, some pattern, light texture |
| Teen/adult casual | Fine, 1–2pt | 60–150 | Layered scenes, decorative fill, mandala elements |
| Adult intricate | Very fine, 0.5–1pt | 150+ | Zentangle density, near-full-page pattern, tiny enclosed cells |

Always specify **closed line work** — every region fully enclosed. Open lines make digital fills bleed across the page and are the top complaint on printed coloring titles.

---

## Coloring book style options

Offer three or four of these, matched to the audience, rather than asking the user to invent a style.

- **Clean cartoon outline** — even weight, rounded corners, friendly. Default for ages 2–7.
- **Storybook line art** — varied line weight, gentle cross-contour, some texture. Ages 8–12.
- **Kawaii / chibi** — oversized head, tiny body, simple sweet faces. Broad appeal, strong on KDP.
- **Botanical illustration** — scientific plate feel, fine even lines, labelled-diagram elegance. Adult.
- **Zentangle / pattern-fill** — subject built from repeating decorative motifs. Adult intricate.
- **Mandala-integrated** — subject sits inside a radial symmetric frame. Adult, standalone-friendly.
- **Art nouveau** — flowing whiplash curves, ornamental borders. Adult, sells well in "beautiful women" niches.
- **Manga / anime line art** — screentone-free, clean inked look, expressive faces. Teen.
- **Whimsical folk** — flattened perspective, decorative naive shapes. Crossover audience.

